. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 *
. 
.         * * For the dependent variable, we construct a gender attitudes index
> , DV_H1, to capture individual beliefs about gender roles in parenting and ho
> usehold responsibilities.This index is based on responses to three statements
>  from the 2018 SOEP individual questionnaire: (1) "Children under 6 suffer if
>  their mother works", (2) "Children under 3 suffer if their mother works", an
> d (3) "It's best if men and women work equally so they can share family and h
> ousehold responsibilities equally." Respondents rated their agreement with ea
> ch item on a scale from 1 ("Do not agree at all") to 7 ("Completely agree"). 
> For interpretability, the first two items were reverse-coded so that higher v
> alues consistently reflect more gender-equal attitudes. We then compute DV_H1
>  as the average of the three items, resulting in a continuous index where hig
> her scores represent more progressive views about gender equality in the fami
> ly domain.
.         
.                 *The independent variable is HasDaughter, a dummy variable co
> ded as 1 if the respondent has least one daughter, 0 otherwise.
.         *We use a tobit regression since dependent variable is censored betwe
> en 1 and 7
.         *an additional note is that differently from the PAP we did not have 
> any religiousness measure in this data, hence we have omitted it from control
>  variables.
. 
.         tobit DV_H1 HasDaughter male pglabnet_clean Age YoungestAge Employed_
> p pgisced11_clean pgisced11_p biokids MarriedStatus, ll(1) ul(7)

